  annotation "note_1771577384199_0", anchor: "Territory seized by force is held by force, and force is not inherited.", text: "The structural comedy is exquisite. A faction that justified its existence by arguing elected councils were too slow to save anyone now watches a splinter faction deploy identical logic to take Terrace Momiou from under them. Threshold did not invent a new argument. They quoted the original back. The Grand Audit Council's formal protest — frozen in administrative ice three layers above the Flipper Accord drafts — uses the word 'illegitimate' without apparent irony. History does not repeat, but it does enjoy a callback.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577384199_1", anchor: "drawing in the Wake-Singers and The Cell before collapsing beyond anyone's control", text: "I noted in the E'frostlings entry that at least two so-called unprovoked orca strikes fell along routes that crossed hunting grounds. 'Drawing in' implies the Wake-Singers arrived uninvited. The ice at Terrace Momiou carries orca thermal signatures in adjacent waters *before* the Breach began. They were already there, watching penguins fight over territory abutting their feeding corridors. The cascade was not an accident of escalation; it was an inevitability of geography. But 'drawing in' reads better.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577384199_2", anchor: "framed as triage, not scholarship", text: "Every faction that studies something forbidden discovers it already owns the perfect euphemism. The Nightshelf called deep-pressure communion 'defensive surveying.' The Covens called corruption indexing 'containment research.' Triage. Splendid.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
